{ id:'enthd-ear-182', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'This **pattern of tympanometry** is observed in which of the following conditions? *(the book prints a tympanogram — compliance in mL against pressure in daPa)*',
  options:['Middle ear effusion','Adhesive otitis media','Otosclerosis','Ossicular discontinuity'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank keys adhesive otitis media — a drum that has healed down onto the promontory cannot move at any canal pressure, so the trace never builds a compliance peak.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` prints the whole pattern menu on one slide: **"Type A: Normal ME pressure, Normal ME compliance — As (with reduced compliance), Ad (with increased compliance); Type B: Non-compliant ME (flat curve); Type C: Negative ME pressure, Good compliance."** `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` says what the instrument is actually reading — the tympanometer tests **"the mobility of the ear drum and the ossicular chain (compliance)"** — and it lists **"Retraction/adhesions"** of the tympanic membrane among the conductive causes, which is adhesive otitis media under the deck’s own wording.\n\n| Option | Expected trace | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Middle ear effusion** | **type B**, flat, low canal volume — the deck’s named example of **"Absent mobility"** | flat as well; see the caveat |\n| **Adhesive otitis media** | **flat, non-compliant** — the drum is fixed to the promontory | **keyed** |\n| **Otosclerosis** | **type As** — a peak at normal pressure but a **shallow** one, because the stapes footplate is fixed while the drum still moves | not flat |\n| **Ossicular discontinuity** | **type Ad** — a peak that is present and **abnormally tall**, a floppy chain | the mirror of As |\n\n**⚠️ This item is decided by the figure, and the figure cannot be re-read here.** The book prints a tympanogram of compliance against pressure; the crop has not been cut and the transcription recorded the axes, not the trace. On tympanometry alone **an effusion and an adhesive drum both give a flat, low-volume type B** *(not taken from the course material — no cached ENT slide prints canal volume)*, so option A is not wrong on the trace; it is excluded by the examiner’s intent. Read the printed graph before trusting the elimination.\n\n**Bottom line: flat means non-compliant, and the two shapes that are NOT flat are the giveaways — a shallow peak is stiffness (As), a tall peak is floppiness (Ad).** Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-202` (type B to effusion), `entqb-ear8-232` (Ad to an atrophic drum), `entqb-ear8-218` (type C to Eustachian tube dysfunction).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A flat, non-compliant tympanogram means the drum and chain cannot move at all — effusion or an adhesive drum; a shallow peak is otosclerosis (As) and a tall peak ossicular discontinuity (Ad).',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.62 (Ear ch.8, Basic audiological evaluation). **The stem depends on a printed tympanogram; no crop exists yet, so no `image` field is set and the trace is described only as far as the transcription recorded it (axes, not shape).** Pattern menu from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; compliance as drum-and-ossicular mobility and "Retraction/adhesions" from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; canal volume is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-183', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'The condition characterized by **the opposite pattern of PTA** has which **tympanometry pattern**? *(the book prints a pure-tone audiogram — both ears, AC and BC symbols)*',
  options:['Type B','Type C','Type As','Type Ad'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The key is type As — normal middle-ear pressure with reduced compliance, the stiff middle ear of otosclerosis.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` defines it in one line: type A is **"Normal ME pressure, Normal ME compliance"**, with **"As (with reduced compliance)"** and **"Ad (with increased compliance)"** as its two variants.\n\n**⚠️ The stem’s back-reference is genuinely ambiguous and is recorded, not resolved.** It says *"the opposite pattern of PTA"* while printing its own audiogram, so there is no antecedent to point at. Only two readings are possible, and only one lands on a printed option:\n\n| Reading | The printed audiogram shows | "The opposite" is | Its tympanogram |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1 — consistent with the key** | a **sensorineural** pattern: AC and BC depressed **together**, no air-bone gap (`L13,14`: *"Abnormal equal AC and BC Thresholds"*) | a **conductive** loss — in an exam, otosclerosis | **type As** ✔ offered |\n| **2** | a **conductive** pattern: AC abnormal, BC normal | a **sensorineural** loss | **type A, normal** ✘ not offered |\n\nSince reading 2 has no answer among the four options, the key forces reading 1. That is an inference from the key, not something the stem states.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:** **type B** is the flat, non-compliant curve of effusion; **type C** is a peak displaced to negative pressure with good compliance, i.e. Eustachian tube dysfunction; **type Ad** is a peak of increased height, a floppy drum or a discontinuous chain — the exact opposite of the stiffness that a fixed stapes produces.\n\n**Bottom line: As = stiff = fixation; Ad = floppy = discontinuity; B = flat = fluid; C = negative = tube.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-232`, `entqb-ear8-218`, `entqb-ear8-202`, and `enthd-ear-182` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Type As is normal middle-ear pressure with a shallow peak — the stiff, fixed middle ear of otosclerosis, and the conductive counterpart of a sensorineural audiogram.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.63 (Ear ch.8). **⚠️ Recorded source defect (house-sweep-ear.md §8): the stem back-references "the opposite pattern of PTA" while printing its own audiogram, so the referent is ambiguous — flagged, not resolved; the key is held as printed.** The figure has no crop yet, so no `image` field is set. Tympanogram types and the AC/BC patterns of conductive, sensorineural and mixed loss are from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; otosclerosis as ossicular fixation from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-185', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'A 22-year-old male patient is told by the otologist that he has **left conductive hearing loss**. This means that his left ear has which of the following characteristics?',
  options:['BC>AC','Rinne negative test','Lateralized Weber’s test to left','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three statements are the same fact written three ways, so the combined option is the answer.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` prints both tuning-fork rules verbatim:\n\n- **Rinne:** *"AC > BC (Positive Rinne test) — Normal (or SNHL)"* and *"BC > AC (Negative Rinne test) — Conductive HL"*. So **BC>AC and "Rinne negative" are two names for one finding** — options A and B cannot be separated.\n- **Weber:** *"Lateralized to better ear — Poorer ear is SNHL"* and *"Lateralized to poorer ear — Poorer ear is conductive"*. The affected ear here is the left, so a conductive loss on the left **lateralizes Weber to the left**.\n\n**Why it works.** A conductive block bypasses the ear’s air route but leaves the cochlea intact, so bone conduction is preserved while air conduction falls — negative Rinne. The same block also stops ambient noise from masking the test ear, so bone-conducted sound is heard **louder** in the diseased ear — Weber goes toward the bad ear. The deck’s third fork test says it a fourth time: *"Pt > Ex (Prolonged Schwabach) — Conductive HL"*.\n\n**Why no single option can be the key:** each of A, B and C is true, so choosing any one of them would leave two correct answers unchosen. This is the deck’s own three-test panel compressed into one item.\n\n**Bottom line: conductive loss = BC>AC = Rinne negative = Weber to the SAME (poorer) ear = Schwabach prolonged.** Sensorineural inverts only the Weber. Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-223` (the same trio asked as Rinne/Weber/Schwabach), `entep-ear-67`, `entqb-ear8-234`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In conductive hearing loss the Rinne is negative (BC>AC), the Weber lateralizes to the affected ear and the Schwabach is prolonged — one lesion, four names.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.63 (Ear ch.8). The printed stem reads "his left hear" and spells the fork tests "Rinne’" and "Webber’s"; the typographic slips are corrected here and the bank’s printed wording recorded. Rinne, Weber and Schwabach rules transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the masking explanation is added and is not stated on the slide. **Q184, the mirror item on the same page, folded into `entep-ear-67`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-186', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'Ear drum mobility is **absent** in which of the following conditions?',
  options:['Tympano-ossicular fixation','Middle ear effusion','Both of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Middle ear effusion — the lecture gives it as the example of ABSENT mobility, in those words.**\n\n`L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` prints a two-line box under tympanometry that this question and its twin split between them:\n\n> *"1- Test the mobility of the ear drum and the ossicular chain (compliance): **reduced mobility: e.g. tympano-ossicular fixation. Absent mobility: e.g. middle ear effusion.**"*\n\n**Why fluid abolishes movement rather than merely damping it.** The middle ear is an air-filled cavity; the drum moves because there is compressible air behind it. Fill the cleft with fluid and there is nothing to compress, so the drum does not move at any canal pressure — which is exactly why an effusion produces the flat **type B** curve, *"Non-compliant ME (flat curve)"* (`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`).\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Tympano-ossicular fixation** | the deck’s example of **reduced**, not absent, mobility — a stiff chain still moves a little, giving the shallow-peaked **type As**. That is the key of the very next question |\n| **Both of the above** | would collapse the distinction the slide is drawing; the whole point of the box is that the two are different degrees |\n| **None of the above** | one option is expressly named on the slide |\n\n**Bottom line: fluid abolishes mobility (flat type B); fixation reduces it (shallow type As).** The pair `enthd-ear-186` / `enthd-ear-187` is the same slide asked twice, once in each direction. Cross-reference: `entqb-ear8-202`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Absent drum mobility means middle ear effusion; reduced mobility means tympano-ossicular fixation — the lecture names each by example.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.63 (Ear ch.8). The reduced/absent mobility box is transcribed verbatim from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; type B as a non-compliant flat curve from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the air-compressibility mechanism is added and is not on either slide. **Deliberately paired with `enthd-ear-187`, which asks the other half of the same box.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-187', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'Ear drum mobility is **reduced** in which of the following conditions?',
  options:['Tympano-ossicular fixation','Middle ear effusion','Both of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Tympano-ossicular fixation — the other half of the same two-line box, and the deck names it as the example of REDUCED mobility.**\n\nThe box and the reasoning are set out in full in `enthd-ear-186`, the twin item printed immediately above this one; they are not repeated here. The one line that decides this question is `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`: *"**reduced mobility: e.g. tympano-ossicular fixation**"*, against *"absent mobility: e.g. middle ear effusion"*.\n\n**What "tympano-ossicular fixation" covers.** The same deck lists the causes under conductive hearing loss: drum **"Retraction/adhesions"** and **"Tympanosclerosis"**, and ossicular chain **"Fixation: Otosclerosis. Tympanosclerosis. Congenital."** In each the chain is stiffened but still intact and still moving a little, which is why the tympanogram keeps a peak at normal pressure and merely loses height — **type As**, *"As (with reduced compliance)"* (`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`).\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Middle ear effusion** | the slide’s example of **absent** mobility — the key of `enthd-ear-186` |\n| **Both of the above** | erases the graded distinction the slide exists to teach |\n| **None of the above** | option A is named on the slide |\n\n**Bottom line: stiff still moves — a little. Fluid does not move at all.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-186`, `entqb-ear8-232` (the Ad mirror), `entqb-ear8-235`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Tympano-ossicular fixation reduces drum mobility and gives a shallow-peaked type As; only effusion abolishes it outright.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.63 (Ear ch.8). *"Reduced mobility: e.g. tympano-ossicular fixation"* and the fixation causes are transcribed from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; *"As (with reduced compliance)"* from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`. **The shared box lives in `enthd-ear-186`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-189', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'A **34-year-old female** patient presents to the ENT clinic with **middle ear effusion**. Which of the following **tympanometry patterns** will be seen in this patient?',
  options:['Type B tympanometry with low basic volume','Type B tympanometry with high basic volume','Type C tympanometry','Type As tympanometry'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Type B with a LOW basic volume — the trace is flat because fluid loads the drum, and the canal volume stays small because the probe is still sealed against an intact membrane.**\n\n**The flat trace is the deck’s own teaching.** `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` defines *"Type B: Non-compliant ME (flat curve)"*, and `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` names the cause: *"Absent mobility: e.g. middle ear effusion"*. So both halves of the question’s first word are sourced.\n\n**The volume is the discriminator, and it is a gap in the course** *(not taken from the course material — no cached ENT slide mentions ear-canal volume; the fact is supplied here and is printed in the other two banks, `entqb-ear8-231` and `entep-ear-204`)*. The tympanometer also reports the air volume it is pressurising. With an **intact** drum that is the external canal alone — roughly 0.6–1.5 mL in an adult — a **low** figure. Through a **perforation** the probe pressurises canal **plus** middle ear and mastoid, so the figure is **high**.\n\n**⚠️ This item is one half of a deliberate pair, and merging the two would invert an answer.** The book prints Q188 immediately above it with **the identical four options**: a **traumatic rupture of the tympanic membrane**, keyed *type B with HIGH basic volume*. Q189 changes only the pathology — an **effusion**, an intact drum — and the key moves to **LOW**. Q188 folded into `entep-mfe5-6`, which prints the same stem and the same key.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Type B, low volume** | **keyed** — flat trace, sealed canal, fluid behind an intact drum |\n| **Type B, high volume** | flat trace but an **open** middle ear: perforation, or a patent grommet. This is Q188’s key |\n| **Type C** | a peak displaced to negative pressure with **good** compliance — Eustachian tube dysfunction, the stage **before** the cleft fills |\n| **Type As** | a peak at normal pressure, merely shallow — stiffness (otosclerosis, tympanosclerosis), not fluid |\n\n**Bottom line: flat says "nothing moves"; the volume says WHY — small means fluid, large means a hole.** Cross-references: `entep-mfe5-6` (the perforation twin), `entqb-ear8-231`, `entep-ear-204`, `entqb-ear8-202`, `entep-mfe5-5`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Effusion and perforation both flatten the tympanogram to type B; the ear-canal volume separates them — low with an intact drum and fluid, high through a perforation.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.64 (Ear ch.8). The printed stem omits "with" before "middle ear effusion"; supplied here. **⚠️ Deliberate pair with Q188 (traumatic perforation, same four options, key HIGH volume), which folded into `entep-mfe5-6` — recorded in house-sweep-ear.md; merging either would invert an answer.** Type B and type C from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`, absent mobility with effusion from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; ear-canal volume is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-190', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'**Absent acoustic reflex** is noticed in which of the following conditions?',
  options:['CHL','SNHL','Psychogenic HL','All of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Conductive hearing loss — the lecture gives it as the example of an ABSENT reflex, in those words.**\n\n`L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` prints the acoustic-reflex box directly under the mobility box, and the two questions on this page split it in half:\n\n> *"2- measurement of acoustic reflex (stapedius cont.): **absent reflex: e.g CHL. Impaired reflex: e.g. SNHL.**"*\n\n**Why a conductive lesion abolishes it outright** *(not taken from the course material — the deck states the result, not the mechanism)*. The reflex is measured as a small change in middle-ear compliance when the stapedius contracts. A conductive lesion breaks the arc **twice**: the stimulus cannot reach the cochlea at adequate intensity through the blocked chain, and even if the muscle did contract the tympanometer could not detect the stiffening of an already fixed or fluid-loaded system. Two failures on one lesion, so the reflex is gone rather than merely reduced.\n\n| Option | Reflex | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CHL** | **absent** | keyed — the deck’s own example |\n| **SNHL** | **impaired** | the cochlea is damaged, so a louder stimulus is needed; the pathway still works. That is the key of `enthd-ear-194` |\n| **Psychogenic HL** | **present and normal** | nothing along the pathway is diseased — the loss is non-organic, which is why an objective test unmasks it *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **All of the above** | ✘ | would erase the absent/impaired distinction the slide exists to draw |\n\n**Bottom line: absent = conductive, impaired = sensorineural, normal = psychogenic — and the last of those three is how an objective test catches a malingerer.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-194`, `entqb-ear8-213`, `entqb-ear8-216`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A conductive lesion abolishes the acoustic reflex; a sensorineural loss only impairs it; a psychogenic loss leaves it intact.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.64 (Ear ch.8). The absent/impaired reflex box is transcribed verbatim from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; the two-failure mechanism and the psychogenic line are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **Deliberately paired with `enthd-ear-194`, the other half of the same box.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-191', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **best test for hearing evaluation in malingerers and children**?',
  options:['PTA','Speech audiometry','Tympanometry','ABR'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**ABR — the lecture names these two groups in its list of ABR indications, almost word for word with the stem.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`: auditory brainstem responses *"are electrical voltages recorded from the brain stem auditory pathway using electrodes placed on the skull"*, and *"**are used to measure the hearing thresholds in newborns, infants, mental retardation and malingerers**"*.\n\n**The reason is the deck’s own division of all hearing testing:** *"**Behavioral** Audiometry: where a behavioral response is needed... (in cooperative children and adults)"* against *"**Electrophysiological** Audiometry: where a physiological reflex or involuntary response is measured... (in Infants/toddlers and uncooperative adults)"*. A child too young to respond and a malingerer unwilling to respond fail on the same axis — neither will give a reliable behavioural answer — so only an objective test serves both.\n\n| Option | Why it fails here |\n|---|---|\n| **PTA** | behavioural: the patient raises a hand. The deck still calls it *"the gold standard of Hearing evaluation"* — but only in someone who cooperates |\n| **Speech audiometry** | behavioural and harder still, since it needs language as well as attention |\n| **Tympanometry** | objective, but it measures **the middle ear**, not hearing: *"Measure 2 Middle Ear features"*. A normal tympanogram says nothing about the cochlea |\n| **ABR** | **keyed** — objective, needs no cooperation, and yields a threshold |\n\n**⚠️ Note the option set.** The same deck calls otoacoustic emissions *"easy and fast"* and *"ideal for neonatal hearing screening"*, and Grade Gain’s `entqb-ear8-215` keys **ABR and OAE together** where OAE is offered. Here it is not, so ABR stands alone. Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-215`, `entep-ear-134`, `enthd-ear-193`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ABR is objective and needs no cooperation, so it gives thresholds in neonates, small children, the uncooperative and malingerers — where PTA and speech audiometry cannot.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.64 (Ear ch.8). The ABR definition, its indication list naming malingerers and newborns, the behavioural/electrophysiological division, "PTA is the gold standard" and the OAE screening slide are all transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`. **Held as a VARIANT of `entqb-ear8-215`**, which prints OAE as an option and keys ABR plus OAE.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-192', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'After conduction of a **speech discrimination test** for a patient with hearing loss, the physician found that his patient has an **excellent speech discrimination score**. This is consistent with which type of hearing loss?',
  options:['CHL','SHL','NHL','MHL'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Conductive hearing loss — the lecture’s three-line rule puts "excellent" opposite CHL and nothing else.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`, *Speech Discrimination Scores*, *"Helps to identify type of hearing loss"*:\n\n| Type | Score on the slide |\n|---|---|\n| **Conductive HL** | **Excellent SDS** |\n| **Sensory HL** | **Good SDS** |\n| **Neural HL** | **Poor SDS** |\n\n**Why a conductive loss spares discrimination** *(not taken from the course material)*: the lesion is an attenuator, not a distorter. Sound arrives quieter but undistorted at a normal cochlea, so once it is made loud enough the patient understands it perfectly — which is why the deck adds that speech audiometry *"provides an idea about the benefit of a hearing aid to restore normal speech discrimination"*.\n\n**Option by option:** **SHL** (sensory, cochlear) scores *good*, not excellent — outer hair cell loss distorts as well as attenuates. **NHL** (neural, retrocochlear — a schwannoma) scores *poor*, and poor scores out of proportion to the audiogram are the classic retrocochlear flag. **MHL** (mixed) carries a sensorineural component by definition, so it cannot score better than that component allows.\n\n**⚠️ The two decks word this differently, and both are recorded.** `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` writes the same rule as *"Scores are **excellent in cochlear lesions** and poor in retrocochlear lesions"* — a two-way split in which a *sensory* loss would count as excellent. `L13,14`’s three-way split, which the bank follows, calls sensory only *good*. Neither is corrected here; the key follows `L13,14`.\n\n**Bottom line: excellent = conductive, good = cochlear, poor = retrocochlear.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-201`, `entqb-ear9-251`, `entqb-ear8-230`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Speech discrimination is excellent in conductive loss, good in cochlear loss and poor in neural loss — so poor scores out of proportion to the audiogram point retrocochlear.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, pp.64–65 (Ear ch.8) — the question prints on p.64 and its key spills to the top of p.65. The three-line SDS table and the hearing-aid line are transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`. **⚠️ Deck-versus-deck divergence recorded, neither corrected: `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` writes the rule as "excellent in cochlear lesions and poor in retrocochlear lesions", a two-way split that would make a sensory loss "excellent".** The attenuator/distortion reasoning is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-193', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'**Acoustic neuroma** is best detected by which of the following **audiological tests**?',
  options:['PTA','Speech audiometry','Tympanometry','ABR'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**ABR — the lecture lists space-occupying lesions among its indications, and a schwannoma is a lesion of the very pathway ABR measures.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`: ABR is *"electrical voltages recorded from the brain stem auditory pathway"*, and *"**also used to measure the neural conduction in neurological disorders (i.e., space occupying lesions and MS)**"*. A vestibular schwannoma sits on the cochlear nerve between an intact cochlea and the brainstem, so it slows conduction along exactly the segment ABR times.\n\n| Option | What it would show | Why it is not "best" |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PTA** | a unilateral, usually **high-frequency SNHL** — `L20) vertigo.pdf` files the schwannoma under *"Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"* | tells you there is a loss and how severe, but **not where**: it cannot separate cochlear from retrocochlear |\n| **Speech audiometry** | **poor** discrimination, out of keeping with the audiogram (`L13,14`: neural HL to poor SDS) | a strong pointer, but a soft one — it raises the suspicion that ABR then tests |\n| **Tympanometry** | **normal** — it *"measures 2 Middle Ear features"*, and the middle ear is healthy | measures the wrong compartment entirely |\n| **ABR** | **delayed wave V and prolonged interwave intervals** *(not taken from the course material)* | **keyed** — the only option that interrogates neural conduction |\n\n**⚠️ Best audiological test is not best test.** For diagnosis the deck goes to imaging: under vestibular schwannoma `L20) vertigo.pdf` prints *"Imaging — Petrous bone and posterior fossa"*, and MRI is the gold standard *(not taken from the course material)*. The stem restricts the field to audiology, and within that field ABR wins.\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear8-214`, `entqb-ear9-251`, `enthd-ear-191`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ABR is the audiological test for a retrocochlear lesion because it times neural conduction; PTA and speech audiometry only raise the suspicion, and MRI makes the diagnosis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.65 (Ear ch.8). Straddles `ent-vertigo` (acoustic neuroma); filed to `ent-audio` because the question is about choosing a test. ABR in space-occupying lesions, the SDS rule and tympanometry as a middle-ear measure are from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the schwannoma under unilateral high-frequency SNHL and "Imaging — petrous bone and posterior fossa" from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; latency criteria and MRI as gold standard are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-194', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'**Impaired acoustic reflex** is noticed in which of the following conditions?',
  options:['CHL','SNHL','Psychogenic HL','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Sensorineural hearing loss — the second line of the reflex box, and the deck names it by example.**\n\nThe box, the mechanism and the full option table are in `enthd-ear-190`, the twin item printed immediately above; they are not repeated here. The line that decides this question is `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`: *"**Impaired reflex: e.g. SNHL**"*, against *"absent reflex: e.g CHL"*.\n\n**Impaired, not absent — and the difference is the teaching point.** In a sensorineural loss the middle ear is normal, so the tympanometer can still register a stapedius contraction; what has changed is that the damaged cochlea needs a **louder** stimulus before the reflex fires. The arc is intact but the trigger has moved, so the reflex is present at raised thresholds rather than gone. In a conductive loss the arc itself is broken at both ends, which is why that one is absent outright.\n\n| Option | Reflex |\n|---|---|\n| **CHL** | **absent** — the key of `enthd-ear-190` |\n| **SNHL** | **impaired** — keyed here |\n| **Psychogenic HL** | normal, since nothing organic is wrong |\n| **All of the above** | ✘ — the slide draws precisely this distinction |\n\n**Bottom line: the reflex needs a mobile middle ear to be measured at all, and a working cochlea to be triggered at normal levels.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-190`, `entqb-ear8-213`, `entqb-ear8-216`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A sensorineural loss impairs the acoustic reflex — it fires at raised levels — whereas a conductive loss abolishes it.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.65 (Ear ch.8). *"Impaired reflex: e.g. SNHL"* transcribed from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; the raised-threshold mechanism is added and is not on the slide. **The shared box lives in `enthd-ear-190`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-195', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'What is/are the **type(s) of hearing loss in which there is an air-bone gap** on PTA?',
  options:['CHL','SNHL','Both of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Conductive hearing loss — an air-bone gap means air conduction is worse than bone conduction, and that is the definition of a conductive lesion.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` sets out the three audiogram shapes:\n\n| Pattern | AC | BC | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Normal** | normal | normal | none |\n| **Conductive HL** | *"AC — Abnormal"* | *"BC — Normal"* | **present** — the gap **is** the diagnosis |\n| **Sensorineural HL** | *"Abnormal **equal** AC and BC Thresholds"* | equally abnormal | **none** — the two curves run together |\n| **Mixed HL** | *"Abnormal **Unequal** AC and BC Thresholds"* | abnormal | **present**, on a depressed BC |\n\n**Why a gap can only be conductive in origin.** Bone conduction bypasses the ear canal, drum and ossicles and drives the cochlea directly. So BC measures the cochlea and nerve; AC measures the cochlea and nerve **plus** the conducting apparatus. The difference between them isolates the conducting apparatus, and nothing else. A cochlear or neural lesion drags both curves down together and leaves no gap.\n\n**Why the other options fail:** **SNHL** has, by the slide’s own words, *equal* AC and BC thresholds. **Both of the above** would require SNHL to produce a gap, which it cannot. **None of the above** contradicts the conductive line.\n\n**⚠️ One caveat worth stating, since the option set hides it.** By the same slide, **mixed hearing loss also shows an air-bone gap** — abnormal *unequal* thresholds. Mixed is not offered here, so CHL is the only correct answer among the four; the key is sound, but "conductive" is not the only pattern with a gap. Cross-references: `entqb-ear8-235`, `entqb-ear8-205`, `enthd-ear-185`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An air-bone gap isolates the conducting apparatus, so it marks a conductive loss — and, on the same slide, the conductive component of a mixed loss. Sensorineural loss gives equal AC and BC thresholds and no gap.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.65 (Ear ch.8). The normal, conductive, sensorineural and mixed audiogram descriptions are transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the reason a gap isolates the conducting apparatus is added and is not on the slide. **Caveat recorded, key not disputed: mixed HL also shows an air-bone gap but is not among the printed options.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-196', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'**ABR** involves all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Auditory nerve','Lateral lemniscus','Cerebral cortex','Inferior colliculus'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The cerebral cortex — the name of the test is the answer. ABR is the auditory BRAINSTEM response, and it stops at the brainstem.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` defines it as *"electrical voltages recorded from the **brain stem** auditory pathway using electrodes placed on the skull"*. `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` makes the boundary explicit by listing the four evoked potentials by level:\n\n| Level | Test |\n|---|---|\n| **Cochlea** | electrocochleography |\n| **Nerve and brain stem** | **auditory brain stem response** |\n| **Subcortical centers** | middle latency response |\n| **Auditory cortex** | cortical auditory potentials |\n\nThe cortex has its **own** test on that list, which is precisely why it is not part of this one.\n\n**The three included options are the brainstem relay, in order** *(not taken from the course material — the deck names the pathway but not its individual generators)*: **auditory nerve** (wave I, the most peripheral generator), **lateral lemniscus** (the ascending tract, around wave IV), **inferior colliculus** (the midbrain relay, wave V — the wave whose latency is read for threshold and for retrocochlear delay).\n\n**Why this matters clinically:** a normal ABR proves the signal reaches the midbrain. It does **not** prove the patient perceives sound — central deafness sits above the ceiling of this test, and needs cortical potentials.\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear8-233` (the same fact over Auditory nerve / Pons / Midbrain / Cortex), `entep-ear-23`, `enthd-ear-191`, `enthd-ear-193`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ABR reads the pathway from the cochlear nerve to the inferior colliculus and stops there; the cortex needs cortical auditory potentials, so a normal ABR does not prove perception.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.65 (Ear ch.8). The ABR definition is from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` and the four-level evoked-potential list from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; the wave generators are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **Held as a VARIANT of `entqb-ear8-233`**, which asks the same exclusion over Pons and Midbrain instead of the lemniscus and colliculus.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-197', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'**Spinning sensation** is mostly linked to a problem localized in which of the following systems?',
  options:['CNS','CVS','Inner ear','Eye'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The inner ear — the deck states the rule in four words: "Vertigo is Vestibular".**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf` sorts every complaint of dizziness into four types — *"Type I Dizziness — **Vertigo**; Type II — Syncope/Pre-Syncope; Type III — Disequilibrium; Type IV — Non-Specific Dizziness"* — and then puts a slide of its own under the heading *"Why Vertigo?"* whose whole content is **"Vertigo is Vestibular"**. A true spinning sensation is the one symptom in the dizziness family that localises, and it localises to the vestibular apparatus.\n\n| Option | What it produces instead |\n|---|---|\n| **CNS** | vertigo too — `L20) vertigo.pdf` lists cerebellar lesions, MS, vertiginous epilepsy, VBI, lateral medullary syndrome and vestibular migraine. But the stem says **mostly**, and the same deck records that *"more than 90% are Benign"* while *"less than 5% are Serious"* |\n| **CVS** | **Type II dizziness** — syncope and pre-syncope, a light-headed greying-out from global hypoperfusion, not a spin |\n| **Inner ear** | **keyed** — the labyrinth: *"SCC (3), angular acceleration, cristae ampullaris"* and *"Vestibule — utricle and saccule, linear acceleration, maculae"* |\n| **Eye** | contributes to balance as one of the three inputs, and its failure gives oscillopsia or unsteadiness — Type III/IV, not rotation |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the key does not say.** It does not deny central vertigo; it says the commonest source of a spin is peripheral. The deck’s own separation of central from peripheral nystagmus — *"Central: not suppressed by visual fixation, could be vertical, could be direction changing, no fatigue"* versus *"peripheral: suppressed by visual fixation, fatigue"* — exists exactly because the minority matters.\n\nCross-references: `entep-ear-46`, `entqb-ear9-241`, `entqb-ear9-240`, `entqb-ear9-236`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'True rotational vertigo is vestibular in origin and mostly peripheral; cardiovascular causes give pre-syncope and visual causes give disequilibrium, not spinning.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.66 (Ear ch.9, Vertigo / dizziness). *"Vertigo is Vestibular"*, the four-type dizziness classification and the 90%-benign figure are transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the central causes list and the central-versus-peripheral nystagmus table from `L20) vertigo.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-198', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'Which of the following drugs cause **temporary** ototoxicity?',
  options:['Aminoglycosides','Cisplatin','Aspirin','Loop diuretics','C&D'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Aspirin and loop diuretics — the two ototoxins whose damage is dose-related and usually recovers when the drug stops.**\n\n**⚠️ Reversibility is a gap in the course, and it is filled here rather than declared.** The ENT decks name the drugs but never grade them: `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` lists only *"Ototoxic drugs: aminoglycosides"* among the causes of sensorineural loss, and `L20) vertigo.pdf` files *"Ototoxicity"* under *"3) Bilateral High Frequency SNHL"*. A grep of all 34 cached ENT decks for *reversible* and *temporary* returns nothing on ototoxicity. The other banks are no better: Grade Gain’s `entqb-ear7-194` prints *"Loop diuretics, salicylates, Quinine and cisplatin are cochleotoxic"* and *"Streptomycin and gentamycin are primary vestibulotoxic"* — the right drugs, sorted by target, not by permanence.\n\n**The distinction, supplied** *(not taken from the course material)*:\n\n| Drug | Mechanism | Course |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Salicylates (aspirin)** | a **functional**, dose-related effect on outer hair cell motility — tinnitus first, then a mild flat or high-frequency loss | **reversible**, typically within days of stopping |\n| **Loop diuretics (furosemide)** | oedema of the **stria vascularis**, disturbing the endocochlear potential rather than killing cells | **usually reversible**; permanent loss is described with rapid intravenous dosing or with an aminoglycoside |\n| **Aminoglycosides** | **destruction of outer hair cells**, from the basal turn upward; cumulative and delayed | **permanent** — hair cells do not regenerate |\n| **Cisplatin** | the same hair-cell destruction, dose-cumulative, often bilateral and high-frequency first | **permanent** |\n\n**Why the combined option and not one of the singles:** aspirin and loop diuretics are both reversible, so keying either alone would leave the other correct and unchosen — the same construction as the *All of the above* items elsewhere in this bank.\n\n**Bottom line: functional insults recover, hair-cell death does not — and that is why aminoglycoside monitoring is done during the course, not after it.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear7-194`, `entqb-ear8-224`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Salicylates and loop diuretics cause a dose-related, usually reversible ototoxicity; aminoglycosides and cisplatin destroy hair cells and the loss is permanent.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.66 (Ear ch.9) — printed in the vertigo chapter but tested as hearing loss, so filed to `ent-hearing`; the vertigo link is `L20) vertigo.pdf`’s "Bilateral High Frequency SNHL — Ototoxicity". **⚠️ Five options are printed, A–E.** *"Ototoxic drugs: aminoglycosides"* is from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; the cochleotoxic/vestibulotoxic split is printed by Grade Gain in `entqb-ear7-194`. **Reversibility is taught by no cached ENT deck and by no other bank — supplied here from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-200', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A patient with **vertigo and auditory symptoms** seeks medical advice. Upon examination, he has an **absent corneal reflex**. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:["Meniere’s disease",'VBI','Acoustic neuroma with compression on the roof of the cerebellopontine angle affecting the 5th CN','Vestibular neuritis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Acoustic neuroma — the corneal reflex is the trigeminal nerve, and a tumour large enough to reach the trigeminal is a cerebellopontine angle mass.**\n\n**Two findings, and each narrows the field.** *Vertigo plus auditory symptoms* puts the lesion in the group `L20) vertigo.pdf` calls peripheral **"with Hearing Loss"**; *an absent corneal reflex* adds a **cranial nerve outside the eighth**, which no purely labyrinthine disease can produce. Only a lesion occupying the angle explains both at once. `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` names the cause of sensorineural loss in one word — *"Neoplastic: **CPA tumour**, glomus"* — and `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` lists **acoustic neuroma** among the causes of tinnitus.\n\n**The corneal reflex** *(not taken from the course material — no cached ENT deck describes the arc)*: afferent **V1**, via the nasociliary nerve; efferent **VII**, closing the lids. A schwannoma arising on the vestibular nerve in the internal auditory canal grows medially into the angle; the trigeminal runs along its upper border, so **V is characteristically the first nerve involved after VIII**, and a lost corneal reflex is its earliest objective sign.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Meniere’s disease** | fits the vertigo and the hearing loss, but is a disorder of endolymph volume confined to the labyrinth — **no cranial nerve signs, ever** |\n| **VBI** | central and vascular, listed by the deck under *"Vascular: vertebrobasilar insufficiency"*; produces brainstem signs in **transient attacks**, not a fixed absent corneal reflex with progressive auditory symptoms |\n| **Vestibular neuritis** | excluded twice over: the deck files it under **"with NO Hearing Loss"**, and it is an isolated vestibular nerve inflammation with no other deficit |\n\n**Bottom line: vertigo with hearing loss AND a fifth-nerve sign is a CPA tumour until imaged.** `enthd-ear-201` gives the bank’s full three-level scheme of what such a tumour compresses. Cross-references: `entqb-ear9-251`, `entep-ear-18`, `enthd-ear-204`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An absent corneal reflex is a fifth-nerve sign; added to vertigo with hearing loss it localises to a cerebellopontine angle tumour, since no labyrinthine disease produces cranial nerve deficits.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.66 (Ear ch.9). The printed option C reads "Cerebellopontine" and "5th CN"; expanded here for readability, wording otherwise unchanged. *"Neoplastic: CPA tumour"* from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`, acoustic neuroma as a tinnitus cause from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf`, the peripheral classification and VBI as a central vascular cause from `L20) vertigo.pdf`. **The corneal reflex arc and the order of cranial nerve involvement are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** ⚠️ `L20) vertigo.pdf` prints vestibular schwannoma struck through — "x Vestibular schwannoma x" — and drops it from both summary slides; recorded, not resolved.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-201', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'Which of the following is a **complication of acoustic neuroma**?',
  options:['Compression on the roof of the CPA affecting the 5th and 6th CNs','Compression of the CPA cavity affecting the 7th and 8th CNs','Compression of the CPA floor affecting the 9th, 10th, 11th and 12th CNs','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — the three options are not rival answers but three stages of one tumour, sorted by which part of the cerebellopontine angle it has reached.**\n\n**The bank’s scheme, level by level:**\n\n| Level | Nerves | When it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CPA cavity** (option B) | **VII and VIII** | earliest and commonest — the tumour arises on the vestibular nerve itself, so VIII is affected from the start and VII runs beside it. `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` traces the facial nerve from the *"pontomedullary sulcus"* where it *"crosses the CPA"*, and lists *"2ry tumors of middle ear and parotid and **CPA**"* among the causes of facial palsy |\n| **Roof** (option A) | **V**, with VI | the tumour grows upward: loss of the corneal reflex, then facial numbness — the finding that decides `enthd-ear-200` |\n| **Floor** (option C) | **IX, X, XI, XII** | a late, large tumour extending downward: dysphagia, hoarseness, shoulder and tongue weakness |\n\n**Why no single option can be the key:** each is a true complication of a sufficiently large tumour, so choosing one would leave two correct statements unchosen. The item is testing whether the growth is understood as progressive rather than as one fixed syndrome.\n\n**⚠️ Two notes on the printed anatomy** *(not taken from the course material — the ENT decks name the CPA but do not divide it)*. The **sixth** nerve is a medial structure at the pontomedullary junction rather than a true angle occupant; it is involved by large tumours and by raised intracranial pressure, so its placement in the roof group is the bank’s convention rather than strict anatomy. And **XII** leaves the medulla below the angle proper. Neither remark touches the key: all three levels are genuine complications, so **D** stands.\n\n**Bottom line: VIII then VII, then V upward, then the lower cranial nerves — the order tracks the size of the tumour.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-200`, `enthd-ear-204`, `entqb-ear9-251`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An acoustic neuroma compresses the CPA in stages — VIII and VII in the cavity, V above, the lower cranial nerves below — so all three levels are complications of the one tumour.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.67 (Ear ch.9). The facial nerve crossing the CPA and CPA tumours as a cause of facial palsy are from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; *"Neoplastic: CPA tumour"* from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`. **The roof/cavity/floor division of the angle and the caution about CN VI and CN XII are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material — no cached ENT deck subdivides the CPA.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-202', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A 35-year-old patient with a **paroxysmal attack of sudden onset of vertigo that lasts up to 20 seconds** and **increases during rotation**, with **normal hearing**. Diagnosis?',
  options:['Labyrinthitis','Vestibular neuritis','BPPV','Vestibular schwannoma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**BPPV — the stem is the lecture’s three-line definition, in order.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` prints benign paroxysmal positional vertigo under *"Peripheral — with NO Hearing Loss"* and defines it with exactly three features:\n\n> *"a) **Vertigo for seconds** — b) **occurring in certain head positions** — c) **Fatigable**"*\n\nThe stem supplies all three: *up to 20 seconds*, *increases during rotation* (that is, provoked by a change of head position, which is what the Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre reproduces deliberately), and *normal hearing*, which is the heading the disease sits under.\n\n| Option | Duration | Hearing | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Labyrinthitis** | **days** | **hearing loss** — the deck files viral labyrinthitis under *"Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"* | fails on both |\n| **Vestibular neuritis** | **days**, *"severe vertigo"* following URTI | normal | fails on duration — it is one continuous storm, not seconds-long attacks |\n| **BPPV** | **seconds**, positional, fatigable | normal | **keyed** |\n| **Vestibular schwannoma** | chronic and progressive | **unilateral high-frequency SNHL** | fails on both |\n\n**The mechanism the duration reflects** *(not taken from the course material)*: otoconia displaced from the utricular macula into a semicircular canal, usually the posterior. They move under gravity when the head position changes, deflect the cupula for as long as they are settling, and then stop — hence seconds, and hence fatigability on repeating the manoeuvre.\n\n**Bottom line: seconds plus position plus normal hearing is BPPV, and nothing else in the peripheral list has that triad.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear9-242`, `entqb-ear9-249`, `entep-mfe5-2`, `entep-ear-106`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'BPPV is vertigo lasting seconds, triggered by a change of head position, fatigable, and with entirely normal hearing.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.67 (Ear ch.9). The three-line BPPV definition and its placement under "with NO Hearing Loss" are transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; the otoconia mechanism is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **Q199 on the same page — the Hallpike-positive management item — folded into `entep-ear-194`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-203', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A 35-year-old male with a history of **sudden severe acute vertigo** with **no cochlear affection and normal hearing**, a history of **URTI**, and nausea. Diagnosis?',
  options:['Labyrinthitis','Vestibular neuritis','BPPV','Vestibular schwannoma'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Vestibular neuritis — the lecture describes it in three lines and the stem reproduces all three.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf`, under *"Peripheral — with NO Hearing Loss — 2) Vestibular neuritis"*:\n\n> *"**Following URTI** — **Severe vertigo** — **for days**"*\n\nThe stem gives the antecedent upper respiratory infection, the severity, the acuteness, and — decisively — **normal hearing**, which is the heading itself. Inflammation is confined to the vestibular nerve; the cochlear division and the cochlea are spared.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Labyrinthitis** | the nearest miss, and the deck sets an ILO to *"differentiate labyrinthitis from vestibular neuronitis"*. Same duration, same viral prodrome — but labyrinthitis takes in the **cochlea** as well, and the deck files it under *"Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"*. **The stem’s "no cochlear affection" is written to exclude it** |\n| **BPPV** | seconds, positional, fatigable — not a continuous storm lasting days |\n| **Vestibular schwannoma** | chronic, progressive, and with a unilateral high-frequency sensorineural loss |\n\n**Bottom line: days of severe vertigo after a cold — hearing intact means neuritis, hearing lost means labyrinthitis.** The two are the same illness separated by one structure. Cross-references: `entqb-ear9-247`, `entep-ear-209`, `entep-ear-65`, `entqb-ear9-248` (its mirror, where hearing IS affected).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Vestibular neuritis is severe vertigo lasting days after an upper respiratory infection with completely normal hearing; add hearing loss and it becomes labyrinthitis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.67 (Ear ch.9). The vestibular neuritis triad, the labyrinthitis heading and the ILO to differentiate the two are transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`. The printed stem repeats "vertigo" in its final clause; tidied here without change of meaning.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-204', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A 55-year-old male complains of **chronic non-episodic vertigo with SNHL**; his **mouth deviates to the right** and he **cannot close the left eye**. Diagnosis?',
  options:['Labyrinthitis','Vestibular neuritis','BPPV','Vestibular schwannoma'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Vestibular schwannoma — three findings that no labyrinthine disease can produce together: a chronic course, a sensorineural loss, and a facial palsy.**\n\n**Reading the facial signs.** A mouth pulled **to the right** with a **left** eye that will not close is a **left lower motor neurone facial palsy** — the paralysed side is dragged across by the intact side. `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` traces the nerve from the *"pontomedullary sulcus"* across the **CPA**, and lists *"2ry tumors of middle ear and parotid and CPA"* and *"Tumors of the CPA"* among the causes of facial palsy. So the same lesion that deafens the eighth nerve has reached the seventh — the *"CPA cavity"* level of `enthd-ear-201`.\n\n**Reading the vertigo.** *Chronic and non-episodic* is the giveaway. A tumour compresses the vestibular nerve slowly, so the brain compensates and the patient reports persistent imbalance rather than attacks. `L20) vertigo.pdf` files vestibular schwannoma under *"Peripheral — with Hearing Loss — Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"*, with *"Imaging: Petrous bone and posterior fossa"* beside it.\n\n| Option | Course | Hearing | Facial palsy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Labyrinthitis** | acute, days | lost | **never** |\n| **Vestibular neuritis** | acute, days | **normal** | **never** |\n| **BPPV** | seconds, positional | normal | **never** |\n| **Vestibular schwannoma** | **chronic** | **unilateral SNHL** | **yes, when it fills the angle** | \n\n**⚠️ A defect in the printed stem, recorded and not disputed.** It reads *"cannot open left eye"*. A facial palsy causes failure to **close** the eye; inability to **open** it is ptosis, a third-nerve or sympathetic sign that does not fit this picture. Read as written the stem is internally inconsistent; read as intended — *cannot close* — it is the classic sign, and the key is unaffected.\n\n**⚠️ And a note on the source.** `L20) vertigo.pdf` prints *"x Vestibular schwannoma x"*, struck through, and omits it from both of its summary slides — the professor appears to have withdrawn it. The disease is still the answer the bank keys, and `L15` and `L19.1` both teach CPA tumours.\n\nCross-references: `enthd-ear-200`, `enthd-ear-201`, `entqb-ear9-251`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Chronic non-episodic vertigo with a unilateral sensorineural loss and a facial palsy is a cerebellopontine angle schwannoma — labyrinthine disease never causes cranial nerve palsies.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.67 (Ear ch.9). **⚠️ Source defect recorded, key held: the stem prints "cannot open left eye", where a facial palsy causes failure to CLOSE the eye.** The facial nerve crossing the CPA and CPA tumours as a cause of palsy are from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the schwannoma under unilateral high-frequency SNHL and "Imaging: petrous bone and posterior fossa" from `L20) vertigo.pdf`, **which prints it struck through and drops it from both summary slides — recorded, not resolved**; central compensation is added and is not on any slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-205', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A 35-year-old female with **vertigo that lasts from 24 minutes to 24 hours** and a **sense of fullness in her ear**. Diagnosis?',
  options:['Labyrinthitis',"Meniere’s disease",'BPPV','Vestibular schwannoma'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Meniere’s disease — hours-long attacks with aural fullness are two members of its tetrad, and no other option on the list is episodic on that timescale.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` gives the disease a slide of its own headed *"Ménière’s disease (**idiopathic endolymphatic hydrops**)"* and prints its **tetrad**: episodic **vertigo**, **fluctuating hearing loss**, **tinnitus** and **aural fullness**. It is filed under *"Peripheral — with Hearing Loss — 1) **Low Frequency SNHL: Ménière’s disease (only)**"* — the word *only* is the deck’s, and it makes a low-frequency sensorineural loss effectively diagnostic.\n\n| Option | Duration | Fullness |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Labyrinthitis** | continuous over **days**, one episode | no |\n| **Meniere’s disease** | **recurrent attacks of minutes to hours**, with symptom-free intervals | **yes — a tetrad member** |\n| **BPPV** | **seconds**, positional | no |\n| **Vestibular schwannoma** | chronic, progressive, non-episodic | no |\n\n**⚠️ A probable misprint in the stem, recorded and not disputed.** *"24 min to 24 hr"* is almost certainly *20 minutes to 24 hours*: the standard diagnostic criterion for a Meniere attack is **20 minutes to 12 hours**, extended to 24 hours in some definitions *(not taken from the course material — no cached ENT deck prints a duration)*. Twenty-four minutes is not a boundary any classification uses, and the digit is shared with the upper limit on the same line. Nothing about the key turns on it: on either reading the attack lasts far longer than BPPV and far shorter than labyrinthitis.\n\n**Bottom line: seconds is BPPV, hours with fullness is Meniere, days is labyrinthitis, months is a schwannoma — duration alone sorts most of the peripheral list.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear9-246`, `entep-ear-18`, `entep-ear-99`, `entep-ear-175`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Episodic vertigo lasting minutes to hours with aural fullness, tinnitus and a fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss is Meniere’s disease — endolymphatic hydrops.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). The tetrad, the "idiopathic endolymphatic hydrops" subtitle and the "Low Frequency SNHL: Ménière’s disease (only)" heading are transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`. **⚠️ Probable misprint recorded, key held: "24 min" is almost certainly 20 minutes; the standard 20-minute-to-12-hour criterion is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-206', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'Which of the following **discriminates a cholesteatoma-induced labyrinthine fistula from BPPV**?',
  options:['Labyrinthine fistula causes auditory manifestations','Labyrinthine fistula causes no vertigo','Labyrinthine fistula is almost always bilateral','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Hearing is the discriminator: a fistula erodes into the labyrinth and takes the cochlea with it, while BPPV leaves hearing untouched.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` puts the two on opposite sides of its central division. BPPV heads the list *"Peripheral — **with NO Hearing Loss**"*. A fistula appears twice on the hearing-loss side: as *"4) **CHL — Third Window Abnormalities**"* and as *"5) **Mixed HL — CSOM with Fistula**"*. Either way the disease is defined by a hearing loss, and BPPV is defined by its absence.\n\n**Both, confusingly, are provoked** — which is why the question exists. BPPV is triggered by a change of head **position**; a fistula is triggered by a change of **pressure**, and the deck gives the test for it: *"**Fistula test** — Siegle’s speculum, or tragal compression"*, which drives pressure through the eroded bone onto the membranous labyrinth and reproduces vertigo and nystagmus. Provocation therefore does not separate them. Hearing does.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Causes auditory manifestations** | **keyed** — conductive or mixed loss, on the deck’s own headings, in an ear that already has chronic suppurative disease |\n| **Causes no vertigo** | **false, and backwards** — vertigo on pressure is the fistula’s cardinal sign, and the whole basis of the fistula test |\n| **Almost always bilateral** | **false** — it follows the cholesteatoma, which is a unilateral disease of one diseased ear; the lateral semicircular canal is the usual site *(see `entqb-ear9-254`)* |\n| **None of the above** | ✘ — option A is true |\n\n**Bottom line: a positive fistula test in a discharging ear is cholesteatoma eroding a canal, and it is a surgical problem — BPPV is neither.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear9-254`, `entqb-ear9-253`, `entqb-ear9-255`, `entep-ear-206`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A cholesteatoma fistula causes hearing loss and pressure-provoked vertigo in one chronically diseased ear; BPPV causes position-provoked vertigo with normal hearing.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). Straddles `ent-csom` (cholesteatoma); filed to `ent-vertigo` because the discrimination asked for is between two causes of vertigo. *"CSOM with Fistula"* under mixed hearing loss, *"Third Window Abnormalities"* under CHL, the fistula test with Siegle’s speculum or tragal compression, and BPPV under "with NO Hearing Loss" are all transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; unilaterality follows from the cholesteatoma and is not stated on the slide.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-207', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'A patient comes to the ENT clinic with a **turning sensation not related to a certain position**, **normal hearing**, and **nystagmus**. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:["Meniere’s disease",'VBI','Acoustic neuroma','BPPV'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Vertebrobasilar insufficiency — and this item is answered by elimination rather than by any positive feature, which is worth saying plainly.**\n\n**Two facts in the stem knock out three options between them**, using `L20) vertigo.pdf`’s own classification:\n\n| Option | Excluded by | The deck’s wording |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meniere’s disease** | **normal hearing** | *"with Hearing Loss — 1) Low Frequency SNHL: Ménière’s disease (only)"* |\n| **Acoustic neuroma** | **normal hearing** | filed under *"Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"* |\n| **BPPV** | **not positional** | *"occurring in certain head positions"* is one of its three defining features |\n| **VBI** | — | the only survivor: *"Central causes — Vascular: **vertebrobasilar insufficiency (VBI)**"* |\n\n**What VBI does fit.** It is a central, vascular cause: transient brainstem ischaemia gives vertigo that is not positional, and it need not involve the cochlea at all, so hearing is typically normal. The nystagmus in the stem is consistent — and the deck gives the features that would confirm a central origin at the bedside: *"Central Nystagmus: 1. **not suppressed by visual fixation**, 2. could be **vertical**, 3. could be **direction changing**, 4. **no fatigue**"*, against *"peripheral Nystagmus: 1. suppressed by visual fixation, 2. fatigue"*.\n\n**⚠️ The weakness of the item, recorded and not disputed.** **Vestibular neuritis fits the stem at least as well** — non-positional vertigo, normal hearing, nystagmus, and the deck’s own second member of the *"with NO Hearing Loss"* group. It is simply not offered. The key is correct **among the printed options**; it is not established by a positive discriminator, and the stem gives none — no duration, no URTI, no direction of nystagmus.\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear9-237`, `entqb-ear9-238`, `entqb-ear9-240`, `enthd-ear-203`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Normal hearing excludes Meniere and schwannoma, and a non-positional history excludes BPPV — leaving VBI, the central vascular cause, as the only fit among the printed options.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). VBI under central vascular causes, the Meniere and schwannoma hearing-loss headings, the BPPV positional criterion and the central-versus-peripheral nystagmus table are all transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`. **⚠️ Recorded, key not disputed: vestibular neuritis fits the stem equally well and is not among the options — the item is decided by elimination, not by a positive feature.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-208', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'In a patient with **Meniere’s disease resistant to anti-vertiginous medications**, what is the best line of management **given that the patient has good hearing capability in the affected ear**?',
  options:['Labyrinthectomy','Gentamycin injection','Vestibular nerve section','A & B are correct'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Vestibular nerve section — the deck names it in the same breath as the hearing it is chosen to protect.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` ends its Meniere slide with a four-step ladder, and the fourth step splits on hearing:\n\n> *"Meniere’s disease treatment: 1) Low salt diet, diuretics — 2) Intra Tympanic CST — 3) Intra Tympanic gentamicin — 4) **Surgery: serviceable — selective vestibular neurectomy; No serviceable hearing: Labyrinthectomy**."*\n\n*Serviceable hearing* is the stem’s *good hearing capability*, and against it the deck prints **selective vestibular neurectomy** — cutting the vestibular division of the eighth nerve while leaving the cochlear division, and therefore the hearing, intact.\n\n| Option | Effect on hearing | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Labyrinthectomy** | **destroys it** — the cochlea is removed with the vestibule | reserved by the deck for *"no serviceable hearing"* |\n| **Gentamycin injection** | **risks it** — gentamicin is vestibulotoxic but also reaches the cochlea; Grade Gain’s `entqb-ear7-194` prints *"Streptomycin and gentamycin are primary vestibulotoxic"*, which is a preference, not a guarantee |\n| **Vestibular nerve section** | **spares it** | **keyed** |\n| **A & B are correct** | pairs the two hearing-destructive options — that is the key of the mirror question, `enthd-ear-209` |\n\n**⚠️ Note where the ladder puts gentamicin.** On the slide it is **step 3**, before surgery — so in a patient who has failed medication it is not an unreasonable next move in general. The stem removes that reading by specifying good hearing, which is precisely the circumstance in which the deck sends the patient to selective neurectomy instead.\n\n**Bottom line: hearing decides the destructive procedure — spare the cochlea if it still works, sacrifice the whole labyrinth if it does not.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-209`, `entep-ear-61`, `entqb-ear9-261`, `entqb-ear9-260`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In medically resistant Meniere’s disease with serviceable hearing the deck’s answer is selective vestibular neurectomy — it ablates balance and spares the cochlea.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). The four-step treatment ladder and the serviceable/no-serviceable-hearing split are transcribed verbatim from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; *"streptomycin and gentamycin are primary vestibulotoxic"* is printed by Grade Gain in `entqb-ear7-194`. **Deliberately paired with `enthd-ear-209`, the same stem with bad hearing.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-209', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'In a patient with **Meniere’s disease resistant to anti-vertiginous medications**, what is the best line of management **given that the patient has bad hearing capability in the affected ear**?',
  options:['Labyrinthectomy','Gentamycin injection','Vestibular nerve section','A & B are correct'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**A and B — once the hearing is already lost, both of the destructive options become available, and the deck names labyrinthectomy for exactly this situation.**\n\nThe treatment ladder and the per-option table are set out in `enthd-ear-208`, the twin item printed immediately above; they are not repeated here. The line that decides this question is `L20) vertigo.pdf`: *"Surgery: serviceable — selective vestibular neurectomy; **No serviceable hearing: Labyrinthectomy**."*\n\n**Why the combined option rather than labyrinthectomy alone.** The only reason to avoid **intratympanic gentamicin** — the deck’s step 3 — is the risk it carries to the cochlea. In an ear whose hearing is already bad that objection disappears, so chemical ablation and surgical ablation are both legitimate, and gentamicin has the advantage of being an office injection rather than an operation *(not taken from the course material — the deck lists both without comparing their morbidity)*. Keying labyrinthectomy alone would leave a correct option unchosen.\n\n**Why C is now wrong.** Selective vestibular neurectomy exists to **preserve hearing**. Where there is no hearing to preserve, it buys nothing over the simpler procedures and imposes a posterior fossa craniotomy *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line: the two questions are one decision — the hearing in the affected ear chooses the procedure, and this pair is how the bank tests it.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-208`, `entep-ear-61`, `entqb-ear9-261`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'With no serviceable hearing, Meniere’s disease resistant to medication is treated by ablating the labyrinth — surgically or chemically with intratympanic gentamicin; nerve section is reserved for hearing worth saving.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). *"No serviceable hearing: Labyrinthectomy"* and the four-step ladder are transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; the comparison of morbidity between chemical and surgical ablation is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **The shared ladder lives in `enthd-ear-208`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-210', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'All of the following cause **vertigo without auditory manifestations except**?',
  options:['BPPV','Vestibular neuritis','VBI=Vertebrobasilar insufficiency','Meniere’s disease'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Meniere’s disease — it is the one entry on this list that is defined by its hearing loss.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` builds its whole classification of peripheral vertigo on this division, so the question is answered by reading the headings:\n\n| Group | Members |\n|---|---|\n| **with NO Hearing Loss** | **1. BPPV — 2. Vestibular neuritis** |\n| **with Hearing Loss — Low Frequency SNHL** | **Ménière’s disease (only)** |\n| **— Unilateral High Frequency SNHL** | viral labyrinthitis |\n| **— Bilateral High Frequency SNHL** | ototoxicity |\n| **— CHL** | third window abnormalities |\n| **— Mixed HL** | CSOM with fistula, otosclerosis, trauma |\n\nTwo of the three distractors are the entire membership of the first group, named by the deck twice over. The third, **VBI**, is not peripheral at all: it sits under *"Central causes — Vascular: vertebrobasilar insufficiency"*, and a brainstem ischaemic attack characteristically gives vertigo, diplopia, dysarthria and drop attacks rather than deafness.\n\nAnd `Ménière’s disease (only)` is the deck’s own phrase — the *only* member of the low-frequency sensorineural group. Its tetrad puts **fluctuating hearing loss**, **tinnitus** and **aural fullness** alongside the vertigo, so three of its four cardinal features are auditory.\n\n**⚠️ One caveat on VBI** *(not taken from the course material)*: where the ischaemia involves the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, which supplies the labyrinth, sudden hearing loss can accompany the vertigo. That is the exception, not the rule, and it does not disturb the key — Meniere is still the only option that **must** have auditory symptoms.\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear9-255`, `entqb-ear9-256`, `enthd-ear-212`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'BPPV, vestibular neuritis and VBI spare hearing; Meniere’s disease is defined by a fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss with tinnitus and fullness.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.68 (Ear ch.9). The full peripheral classification, the "with NO Hearing Loss" pairing, "Ménière’s disease (only)" and VBI under central vascular causes are all transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; the AICA caveat is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-211', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'All of the following cause **intermittent vertigo except**?',
  options:['BPPV',"Meniere’s disease",'Labyrinthitis','Vestibular neuritis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Vestibular neuritis — it is a single continuous storm lasting days, not a series of attacks.**\n\n`L20) vertigo.pdf` describes it in three lines and every one of them is continuous: *"**Following URTI** — **Severe vertigo** — **for days**"*. There is one episode; the patient is prostrate through it and then recovers as central compensation takes over. Nothing about it recurs in bouts.\n\n| Option | Pattern | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BPPV** | **recurrent attacks of seconds**, each provoked by a head position, fatigable | *"a) Vertigo for seconds b) occurring in certain head positions c) Fatigable"* |\n| **Meniere’s disease** | **recurrent attacks** with symptom-free intervals; the deck’s own word for its vertigo is **episodic** | the Meniere tetrad slide |\n| **Labyrinthitis** | **intermittent in its circumscribed form** — the endpoint bank prints exactly this presentation: *"a 35-year-old male with a history of acute suppurative otitis media now complains of **intermittent vertigo**, nystagmus and sensorineural hearing loss"*, keyed labyrinthitis (`entep-ear-206`) | `L20`, plus the other bank’s printed key |\n| **Vestibular neuritis** | **one continuous episode over days** | **keyed as the exception** |\n\n**⚠️ Worth naming the tension, since it is the reason this item is harder than it looks.** *Viral* labyrinthitis, as `L20` presents it, runs the same continuous course as neuritis over days — the two differ by the cochlea, not by the rhythm. The key is defensible because labyrinthitis has a second face that neuritis does not: the circumscribed or serous form complicating middle ear disease, which the corpus’s other bank prints as intermittent. **Preferring that printed answer to a general-knowledge argument is what settles it.** The key is held as printed.\n\nCross-references: `entep-ear-206`, `entqb-ear9-248`, `enthd-ear-203`, `enthd-ear-212`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'BPPV, Meniere and circumscribed labyrinthitis recur in attacks; vestibular neuritis is one continuous episode lasting days.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.69 (Ear ch.9). The vestibular neuritis triad, the BPPV three-line definition and the Meniere tetrad are transcribed from `L20) vertigo.pdf`; **intermittent vertigo as a presentation of labyrinthitis after suppurative otitis media is taken from the endpoint bank’s printed key at `entep-ear-206`, preferred to outside knowledge under the standing rule.** Recorded, key not disputed: viral labyrinthitis as `L20` describes it is as continuous as neuritis.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-212', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-vertigo',
  stem:'All of the following cause **continuous vertigo except**?',
  options:['Acoustic neuroma','Vestibular neuritis',"Meniere’s disease",'None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Meniere’s disease — its vertigo is episodic by definition, with well periods in between; the other two are continuous.**\n\n| Option | Course | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Acoustic neuroma** | **continuous** — a slowly growing tumour produces persistent imbalance and unsteadiness rather than attacks; `L20) vertigo.pdf` files vestibular schwannoma under *"Unilateral High Frequency SNHL"*, and the presentation the bank itself writes for it is *"chronic non-episodic vertigo"* (`enthd-ear-204`) |\n| **Vestibular neuritis** | **continuous** — *"Following URTI — Severe vertigo — **for days**"*: one unbroken episode |\n| **Meniere’s disease** | **episodic** — the tetrad slide’s vertigo is recurrent, each attack lasting minutes to hours and separated by symptom-free intervals (`enthd-ear-205`) | **keyed as the exception** |\n| **None of the above** | ✘ — one option is genuinely the odd one out |\n\n**This item is the exact complement of `enthd-ear-211`**, printed on the same page: there the question asks which is **not** intermittent and the answer is neuritis; here it asks which is **not** continuous and the answer is Meniere. Read together they are one axis with the two extremes tested from each end, and Meniere and neuritis sit at opposite ends of it.\n\n**Bottom line: episodic with hearing symptoms is Meniere; continuous over days is neuritis or labyrinthitis; continuous over months with a progressive unilateral loss is a schwannoma.** Duration and rhythm do most of the diagnostic work in this chapter — `L20) vertigo.pdf` puts *"a) Duration"* first among the history items it lists under diagnosis.\n\nCross-references: `enthd-ear-211`, `enthd-ear-204`, `enthd-ear-205`, `entqb-ear9-246`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Meniere’s vertigo comes in discrete attacks with symptom-free intervals; vestibular neuritis and an acoustic neuroma both produce continuous symptoms, over days and over months respectively.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.69 (Ear ch.9). Option B is printed "Vestibular neurtitis"; the typographic slip is corrected here. **⚠️ SOURCE DEFECT — Q212 IS PRINTED TWICE ON BOOK p.69 as two entirely different questions.** This entry is the **first** printing, *"All of the following cause continuous vertigo except?"*; the second printing is a different question altogether and folded into `entep-ear-198`, so it has no entry of its own. The plain id `enthd-ear-212` is therefore unambiguous. Recorded in house-sweep-ear.md §1. The vestibular neuritis triad, the Meniere tetrad, the schwannoma heading and *"a) Duration"* under history are all from `L20) vertigo.pdf`.' }
